## The slot at the top

Every chat starts with a system prompt (Ch 21), placed in a slot of the chat template (Ch 13) ahead
of your message. Chat models were tuned on examples of an assistant following instructions, and on
people's ratings of which replies were better.<Cite id="ouyang2022" /> So instructions there tend
to be followed. But what kind of instructions help?

Here is Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct answering the same question under three system prompts. The
highlighted part is the only text that changes:

<SystemPrompts />

## Specific beats grand

The specific instructions worked: two short sentences and an everyday example, shorter than the
default answer. The persona, "a world-famous physicist", made the answer more than twice
as long and no better: it added claims that are muddled, like most blue light being "scattered away
from us towards the edges of the sky".

The sums tell the same story. Telling the model it is "a brilliant mathematician who never makes
mistakes" did not make it one: it got 3 of the 8 right, against 5 of 8 with the default system
prompt. A large study found the same across 162 personas and 2,410 factual questions: adding a
persona did not improve accuracy on average, and which persona helped on which question was largely
random.<Cite id="zheng2024" />

That fits how the model works. A persona changes which documents the text resembles, so it changes
tone, length, and vocabulary. It doesn't add knowledge or skill the model lacks. Instructions that
say what you actually want (who the reader is, how long, what format) give the model something it
can follow.

<RecapCard>

- The system prompt is text in a slot at the start of the chat, read like any other text.
- Clear, specific instructions about audience, length, and format are followed well.
- A persona changes style, not ability; "you never make mistakes" doesn't make it so.

</RecapCard>

